<BlogPost slug="prime-iptv-apps-tested">
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Who wrote this:</strong> we are Prime IPTV, so this is our own service. This is a first-party write-up of the apps we tried with our own trial, not an independent review. Test your own device with the <a href="/free-trial/">24-hour free trial</a>.</p></div>

  <p>Prime IPTV is a subscription service, so you watch it through a player app. During our own 24-hour test we tried four apps on two kinds of device. Here is what we used, what happened and what we did not try.</p>

  <h2>The apps we tested</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Device</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>The app supplied with the subscription</strong></td><td>Not recorded</td><td>It worked well. The app name and the device were not recorded in our notes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>TiviMate</strong></td><td>TV</td><td>Worked without problems.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>IBO Player</strong></td><td>TV</td><td>Worked without problems.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>An IPTV app on iPhone</strong></td><td>iPhone</td><td>Worked very well. Our notes say MyTVOnline 3, but we could not confirm the exact app name, so treat that name as unconfirmed.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>This was one short session on a premium server at about 60 Mbps at peak hours. The TV models and the exact date were not recorded. See the <a href="/blog/prime-iptv-trial-test/">full 24-hour test</a> for the channel, sports and support results.</p>

  <h2>What we did not test</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>IPTV Smarters Pro, VLC, Kodi and other players</li>
    <li>Amazon Fire TV, Roku, Apple TV, Samsung and LG apps, one by one</li>
    <li>Any device other than the TV and iPhone above</li>
    <li>Catch-up in each app, since it did not work in our test</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Other players should work too if they accept the login we give you, but we have not tested them, so do not treat that as a promise. Use the trial to check yours.</p>

  <h2>How to choose a player for Prime IPTV</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with your device.</strong> Pick a player that exists for it. Our <a href="/setup/">setup guides</a> cover Fire TV, Android TV, Apple TV and more.</li>
    <li><strong>Check which login you use.</strong> Players differ in whether they take Xtream Codes or an M3U link. Read <a href="/blog/iptv-m3u-vs-xtream-codes/">M3U vs Xtream Codes</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Install the player before you request the trial.</strong> That saves trial hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Test the guide.</strong> If you care about the TV guide or catch-up, check them in that exact player.</li>
    <li><strong>Download only from official stores.</strong> Do not install players from random links.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For a wider comparison, see our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-iptv-players/">best IPTV players and apps</a>.</p>

  <h2>Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>Does Prime IPTV work with TiviMate?</h3>
  <p>Yes, it worked without problems in our test, on a TV.</p>
  <h3>Does Prime IPTV work with IBO Player?</h3>
  <p>Yes, it worked without problems in our test, on a TV.</p>
  <h3>Is there a Prime IPTV app for iPhone?</h3>
  <p>We used an IPTV player app on an iPhone and it worked well. We did not confirm its exact name, and that app is a third-party player, not ours.</p>
  <h3>Is Prime IPTV itself a player app?</h3>
  <p>No. Prime IPTV is the subscription service. A player app only displays what a service gives it. Read <a href="/blog/iptv-service-vs-iptv-player-app/">the difference</a>.</p>
</BlogPost>
